BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:AWS User Group Mixtli|Sesión 4: IA Responsable: Seguridad y Gobie
 rno|Online
DTSTART:20260904T183000
CATEGORIES:ai,cloud,frontend,web
DESCRIPTION:AWS User Group Mixtli\nEsta es nuestra **cuarta sesión de pre
 paración para la certificación AWS Certified AI Practitioner**. En esta 
 ocasión hablaremos sobre la importancia de desarrollar y utilizar la IA d
 e manera responsable y segura\, considerando aspectos como la confianza\, 
 la protección de datos y la gestión de riesgos.\nTambién revisaremos de
  forma general cómo AWS aborda la seguridad\, la gobernanza y el cumplimi
 ento en soluciones de IA\, así como algunas buenas prácticas para manten
 er nuestros sistemas protegidos\, trazables y alineados con los requisitos
  de la organización.\n\n📖 **Material oficial de estudio:**\n\n* [AWS A
 rtificial Intelligence Practitioner Learning Plan](https://skillbuilder.aw
 s/learning-plan/G8ENMJ5QBE/aws-artificial-intelligence-practitioner-learni
 ng-plan-espaol-latam/WGQ51HKDAP)\n* [Guía oficial del examen](https://d1.
 awsstatic.com/onedam/marketing-channels/website/aws/es_ES/certification/ap
 proved/pdfs/docs-ai-practitioner/AWS-Certified-AI-Practitioner_Exam-Guide.
 pdf)
LOCATION:Online event\,
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:México City AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup|Sept
  9 - Physical AI Has a Data Problem. It Isn't Collection Workshop|Online
DTSTART:20260909T100000
CATEGORIES:ai,backend,data,frontend,web
DESCRIPTION:México City AI\, Machine Learning and Computer Vision Meetup\
 nThis workshop goes from raw recording to curated corpus. We'll cover what
  MCAP is and why it's built that way\, tour real Physical AI datasets acro
 ss driving\, aquatic\, and forest robots\, and open an episode with every 
 sensor synced—including channels nothing knows how to decode.\n\n**Time\
 , Date and Location**\n\nSep 09\, 2026\n9:00 AM - 10:00 AM PST\nOnline. **
 [Register for the Zoom!](https://voxel51.com/events/document-visual-ai-wor
 kshop-september-9-2026)**\n\nPhysical AI still relies on familiar computer
  vision tasks—detection\, segmentation\, depth\, tracking. What's change
 d is the data unit: no longer a single image and label\, but an episode—
 a dozen sensors ticking on independent clocks for minutes\, with no frame 
 boundaries.\n\nMost computer vision tooling assumes the old unit and break
 s on the new one.\n\nThat's why Physical AI teams end up with buckets of .
 mcap files nobody can characterize. Recording is cheap\, so logs pile up f
 aster than anyone curates them. Ask what's actually in there—which tasks
 \, which conditions\, how many failures and of what kind—and the honest 
 answer is usually a shrug.\nMCAP has been ROS 2's default log format since
  Iron\, and as of [FiftyOne 1.19](https://docs.voxel51.com/index.html) it 
 opens natively: cameras\, LiDAR\, GPS\, IMU\, and logs on one shared timel
 ine\, alongside your images and video.\n\nWe'll tackle quality\, the harde
 r half: what smoothness\, sensor-health\, and outlier metrics actually mea
 sure\, where each falls short\, and how to turn a score into a defensible 
 decision.\n\nYou'll leave knowing how to load your own recordings\, query 
 a whole corpus instead of a single file\, and which quality signals to tru
 st for which job.
LOCATION:Online event\,
END:VEVENT
